shed before it could transform anything. Adding `import collections` at the top of `transform` fixes this. The rule itself has not changed.\n\n**The rule.**\n- The grid consists of two solid rectangles, one of colour 8 and one of colour 1, with red (colour 2) blocks sitting inside them.\n- Each red block belongs to the region whose colour surrounds it most. I find this by counting the non-red cells next to the block.\n- Blocks in the **8 region slide left** until their left column reaches the left edge of the 8 rectangle.\n- Blocks in the **1 region slide right** until their right column reaches the right edge of the 1 rectangle.\n- Rows and shapes stay the same, and the cells a block leaves behind take the region's colour.\n- Each block moves on its own. In pair 1 this is visible: the 2×2 block and the single cell in the 1 region both end flush at x=10, even though they started at different columns.\n\n**Expected results on the tests.**\n- **Test 1** (1 on top, 8 below): the pair at row 1 goes to x=7..8, the cell at (2,5) goes to x=8, the 2×2 block goes to x=0..1, and the cell at (7,1) goes to x=0.\n- **Test 2** (1 on the left, x=0..5; 8 on the right, x=6..10):\n  - In the 1 region, the blocks end flush at x=5.\n  - In the 8 region, the blocks start at x=6.\n\n**Checkpoints.** The program emits one checkpoint after each region's blocks have moved. The second checkpoint is the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    import collections\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    objs = objects(g, bg=None)\n    reds = [o for o in objs if o[\"colour\"] == 2]\n    groups = {1: [], 8: []}\n    for o in reds:\n        cells = set(o[\"cells\"])\n        cnt = collections.Counter()\n        for (y, x) in cells:\n            for dy, dx in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):\n                ny, nx = y + dy, x + dx\n                if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W and (ny, nx) not in cells and g[ny, nx] != 2:\n                    cnt[int(g[ny, nx])] += 1\n        if not cnt:\n            continue\n        c = cnt.most_common(1)[0][0]\n        groups.setdefault(c, []).append(o)\n    for c in (8, 1):\n        if c not in groups or not groups[c]:\n            continue\n        ys, xs = np.where(g == c)\n        rx0, rx1 = xs.min(), xs.max()\n        for o in groups[c]:\n            for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n                out[y, x] = c\n        for o in groups[c]:\n            y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n            shift = (rx0 - x0) if c == 8 else (rx1 - x1)\n            for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n                out[y, x + shift] = 2\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Slid every red block in the colour-{c} region \"\n             f\"{'left' if c == 8 else 'right'} to that region's edge, filling vacated cells with {c}.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Blocks in the **8 region slide left** until their left column reaches the left edge of the 8 rectangle."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Blocks in the **1 region slide right** until their right column reaches the right edge of the 1 rectangle."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"I find this by counting the non-red cells next to the block."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Test 1** (1 on top, 8 below): the pair at row 1 goes to x=7..8, the cell at (2,5) goes to x=8, the 2×2 block goes to x=0..1, and the cell at (7,1) goes to x=0."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"f4d908c2bb91e07bcc8ee73907014db78d3b19ad770335e754020c5e2d4d7e3c","task_sha256":"f0082f55df2a8d84f80643a4ac2cc3f9847b56be2b3b4d50c8f6781955fcb04c","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
